\f1\fs22 Instead of being executed, Emil Kalheri decided he\'92d rather go to the pub.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There are, perhaps, braver rebels against the machinery of fate, but few so unfussy. Emil had just finished turning a man\'92s head into a sort of visceral postmodern fruit bowl and made the grimmest of gestures towards disguising it as suicide.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It went down exactly as Agent Jones said. Emil sauntered into the office tower just after the witching hour, his stride advertising self entitlement, insurance fraud, or both. The entry system, in thrall to fashion, clocked the midnight blue suit\'97supplied by Agent Jones and tailored, if not to Emil\'92s taste, then at least to someone's idea of expensive\'97and granted him all-access. The luxurious Airborne-only elevator parted its golden jaws and gobbled him upward, two hundred floors closer to nirvana or vertigo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The office floor was lit by the faint glow of passing hover-copts and neighbouring towers. Emil marvelled at the crystalline clarity of the night air high above the toxic bone-eating clouds of the Layer. He halted at reception\'92s panoramic windows, his gaze arrested by a celestial vision\'97a golden, milky light that defied explanation. \'93You idiot, Emil,\'94 he chided himself. \'93That\'92s the moon.\'94 He\'92d never seen it before, and it seemed unfairly famous.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Travis Teh\'92s was the only office with lights on at this hour and altitude. When threatened with the shotgun, he was all guppy lips and jelly limbs, handing over the item Emil came for. As per instructions, Emil shot him anyway, with the barrels shoved between Teh\'92s jaw and Adam\'92s apple. Teh even obliged Emil\'92s request to hold the gun\'92s grip as Emil stroked the trigger. Perhaps Teh thought it an opportunity to yank the gun away. He was wrong.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Emil felt powerful. So powerful, he experienced an overwhelming urge to pee. He found the office\'92s opulent bathroom, stood at the urinal, and caressed himself for longer than functionally required. Even the pain of his testicular cancer felt tamed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax you!\'94 Emil cursed his semen stones. \'93You won\'92t get the chance to kill me!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An unknown assassin would have that honour once Emil delivered the item. Agent Jones promised it would be quick and clean. Emil cared little; any death would be more dignified than his own gonads eating him from the inside out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Emil triumphantly zipped his fly and decided against washing his hands.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A few blocks from the office, the Chapelle Boogie Bar was open 24/7 to the reckless, the shiftless, and the deservedly underprivileged. With the satisfaction of a high-stakes gambler after a big win, Emil perched himself on a stool, his suit drawing stares from the bartender.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You sure you\'92re in the right place?\'94 the barkeep asked, mistrusting Emil\'92s suit the way dogs mistrust vacuum cleaners.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m exactly where I want to be,\'94 Emil said, baring his armpit for a consumer scan. \'93Your best tequila. And keep it coming.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender squinted at one of the bar\'92s Grogify tablets. \'93Your credit runs dry before the bottle does. And it\'92s half empty.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Fit for purpose, my good soul.\'94 Emil would drink his account dry, pickled-happy. The Blank Contract he signed guaranteed his family would be free of money woes. A momentary wave of sorrow took him. Peggy and the kids would be so much better off without him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Shot down, glass forward. After the second, Emil remembered his little errand and fished out the disc, twice the width of his thumb. He screwed his face into a pinch of unanswered questions. It gave no clue it was worth killing for\'97dull and tough and bendable, the colour of last year\'92s promises, not metal but... rubber? Rawhide? Crocodile, perhaps, though Emil had never seen one outside children\'92s books.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the ambiguous light, the disc glinted. Or Emil thought it glinted. He downed another shot and waited for it to glint again. When it inertly refused, he bounced it in his palm, testing its weight. Then he tossed it up like a coin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Emil\'92s stool trembled, his tequila splashed, bar-lights swung. Bottles leapt from shelves. Customers shrieked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Emil closed his hand around the disc. Everything quieted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What the tax was that?\'94 the bartender blurted. \'93Earthquake? Here?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Emil shrugged. \'93That, or the tequila's stronger than your insurance.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With his account drained and his brain steeped in fermented cactus juice, Emil staggered into the toxic night. The air smelled like boiled cabbage, wet cats, and fried noodles wrapped in fetid underwear. Agent Jones would be expecting him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That's when Emil dropped dead. It wasn't as quick or clean as Special Agent Jones promised. Not a sniper, not his cancerous sperm-rocks. His fate lay at the crossroads of alcoholism and unaffordable medical insurance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Emil died of a heart attack.
